Description: Visible texts through gettext, clock locale from LC_TIME
 The login screen of Querencia Desktop shows ReGreet in the system
 language. Visible texts (labels, buttons, tooltips, error messages) go
 through glib::dgettext with the domain "querencia-anmeldung"; the catalog
 comes from po/de.po of the Querencia part of this source package.
 Untranslated texts stay English. A rejected password (PAM AUTH_ERR) gets
 a plain message instead of "Login failed: Pam_authenticate: AUTH_ERR".
 .
 An expired password (PAM NEW_AUTHTOK_REQD, e.g. after "chage -d 0")
 cannot be changed on the login screen: greetd 0.10.3 never calls
 pam_chauthtok, it ends the login with "pam_acct_mgmt: NEW_AUTHTOK_REQD"
 right after PAM's own message, which therefore vanished at once.
 Instead of "Login failed: Pam_acct_mgmt: NEW_AUTHTOK_REQD" the screen
 says that the password has expired and points to the text console
 (Ctrl+Alt+F1); the message stays for a minute instead of five seconds.
 .
 greetd calls no setlocale, so PAM prompts and PAM info and error messages
 always arrive in English. They go through tr_pam: first the
 "querencia-anmeldung" catalog, then the "Linux-PAM" catalog of the system
 (e.g. "You are required to change your password immediately ...").
 .
 The clock takes its locale from LC_ALL, LC_TIME or LANG ("de_DE.UTF-8"
 -> de_DE). sys-locale ignores LC_TIME and returns "de-DE", which
 chrono::Locale does not parse, so the clock fell back to en_US.
 .
 No new crates; Cargo.lock is unchanged (glib comes with gtk4/relm4).
